const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Literature Review and Gap Analysis Service","serviceType":"Literature review writing, critical appraisal and research gap analysis","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/literature-review-and-gap/","description":"Reproducible searches across PubMed, Embase, Scopus and Web of Science, with the gap stated as a count rather than “more research is needed”.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How should a literature review be structured?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Thematically, and almost never chronologically. The structure that fails is the one that mirrors the order you read things in — a paragraph per study, each beginning with an author's name. The structure that works groups the literature into the three to six themes the field actually argues about, and within each theme states what is agreed, what is disputed, what the evidence rests on and where it stops. Every section should end by connecting back to your own objectives, so that by the closing paragraph the gap has already been demonstrated rather than announced. A useful test: if the studies could be reordered without the argument changing, there is no argument yet."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between a literature review and a gap analysis?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A literature review says what is known. A gap analysis says what is not, and does it precisely enough to act on. They are usually sold together because the second is impossible without the first, but they are different pieces of work and the second is where most reviews stop short. A real gap analysis names four things: which population and which outcome have never been studied together, where the evidence exists but rests on designs too weak to support the claims made from it, where studies contradict each other, and which group has been excluded from all of it. Each one is then written as a question a study could answer, with the design that would answer it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which type of review do I actually need?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Work backwards from what it has to survive. A thesis chapter usually needs a critical or integrative review, unless your examiner specified systematic — in which case nothing else will do, and finding that out late is expensive. A grant background needs a scoping review with a strong gap analysis, because the panel is assessing the gap rather than the synthesis. A clinical evaluation report under EU MDR needs a systematic, documented search because a notified body will audit the method. A journal submission needs whatever type the journal publishes, which for review articles is often narrower than authors expect. And if you want to publish the review itself rather than use it as background, systematic or scoping is usually the only route, because narrative reviews are increasingly hard to place."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I select and organise studies efficiently?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Write the inclusion and exclusion criteria before you start reading, in enough detail that two people would apply them the same way — that single step saves more time than any tool. Then search with controlled vocabulary rather than keywords, because MeSH and Emtree find what a phrase search misses, and follow it with citation searching in both directions on the papers that matter most. Manage everything in EndNote, Mendeley or Zotero from the first search, not from the point at which it becomes unmanageable. And organise into a matrix rather than into folders: one row per study, one column per attribute you will later want to group by. Folders let you find a paper; a matrix lets you see a pattern."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I critically analyse and synthesise rather than summarise?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Summarising describes each study in turn. Synthesis makes the studies talk to each other, and the mechanical difference is what your sentences are about. If a sentence's subject is an author, you are summarising; if its subject is a finding, a disagreement or a limitation shared across several studies, you are synthesising. Critical analysis goes one step further and asks what each study's design can actually support — whether the sample justifies the generalisation, whether the outcome measured is the outcome claimed, whether the comparator was fair, and who funded it. The useful habit is to write the appraisal into the matrix as you read, because reconstructing it later from memory is where most reviews turn back into summaries."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What goes wrong most often in literature reviews?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Seven things, in roughly this order. No focus, so the review describes a field rather than building towards a question. A structure that follows reading order rather than argument. Summarising where appraisal was needed. Literature that stops three years short, usually because the search was run once and never re-run. A search that cannot be reproduced from what the methods say, which is fatal in anything systematic. Referencing that does not match the studies described. And a conclusion that does not connect to the research objectives — a review that ends without the reader knowing what it was for. Six of the seven are set before any writing begins."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many sources should a literature review include?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"There is no number, and any service that quotes you one is pricing rather than advising. What matters is whether the search was complete against its own stated criteria. A narrow, well-defined clinical question might have forty eligible studies and a review that cites sixty is padding; a broad scoping question might return three hundred and citing forty would be a selection problem rather than an editorial choice. Where a word limit forces a cut, the honest approach is to state the criteria that decided what was cited and supply the full matrix as supplementary material — so the reader can see that the selection was systematic rather than convenient."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What if the gap analysis finds that my question has already been answered?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Then we tell you, in the first week, and that is the most valuable thing this service does even though it is the least welcome. It is also rarely the end of the project. A question answered in one population is often open in another; a question answered by three weak studies is open to a well-designed one; a question answered in 2016 is open again if the technology or the standard of care has moved. What usually happens is that the question narrows rather than disappears — and a narrow question with a demonstrated gap is a considerably stronger proposal than the broad one you started with."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you work from a draft I have already written?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and a good deal of our work arrives that way. The first deliverable is a written critique rather than a revision, because it has to say honestly what is repairable. Structure, synthesis, appraisal, referencing and language all are — a review organised study by study can be restructured around themes in days. What cannot be repaired by editing is a search that was never systematic, or literature that stops before the last three years; both have to be re-run, and a rewrite that papers over either will fail with the next reader for exactly the same reason it failed with the last. Expert editing then handles the rest: clarity, logical flow, transitions, redundancy, consistent terminology and academic tone."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who is the author, and how is AI use declared?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You are. Authorship under ICMJE requires substantial contribution to conception or design, or to acquisition, analysis or interpretation; drafting or critically revising; approving the final version; and agreeing to be accountable for it. Pubrica supports all four but takes none of them in your place, and we do not accept authorship on client work. Where our contribution should appear in the acknowledgements we say so and draft the wording. On artificial intelligence: any AI-assisted searching, screening or drafting is declared in the methods, with what was used and for which step, because ICMJE and COPE both require it now and an undeclared tool found afterwards is treated as a research integrity issue rather than a matter of style."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How recent do the sources have to be?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"There is no rule, and the five-year convention some supervisors quote is a heuristic rather than a standard. What matters is whether the literature has moved. In a field where the measurement technique changed in 2019, a 2016 paper may be describing something no longer comparable; in a stable field, the 1998 paper may still be the definitive one and leaving it out looks like you have not read the area. The defensible approach is to state the date range and justify it in a sentence, then include the older work that is foundational and say why it is. An examiner objects to an unexplained cut-off far more often than to an old citation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I include preprints, theses and conference abstracts?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, with the status labelled. Excluding them entirely introduces exactly the publication bias a review is supposed to expose, and in fast-moving fields the preprint is often where the finding first appeared. What is not acceptable is presenting a preprint as though it were peer reviewed. We record publication status as a field in the extraction matrix, mark it in the evidence table, and where a conclusion depends materially on non-peer-reviewed evidence we say so in the limitations. Conference abstracts carry a particular caution: the published paper, when it appears, not infrequently reports different numbers, and the abstract rarely contains enough method to appraise."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is a literature review in a grant proposal different from one in a thesis?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Length and purpose, which change almost everything. A thesis review establishes that you have command of the field and can take a position within it; it can afford to be comprehensive and is read by people who will test its edges. A grant review has perhaps three hundred words, is read by someone deciding whether to keep reading, and exists to make one gap unavoidable. The thesis version cannot be cut down to the grant version — the selection criterion is different, not just the word count. Written properly, the grant version cites the three papers that close off every alternative explanation and nothing else."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I avoid plagiarism and self-plagiarism in a literature review?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A review is more exposed to both than any other section, because so much of it is other people’s work restated. Two practical rules do most of the work. Extract into a structured matrix rather than into prose, so that what you write later comes from fields you filled in rather than from sentences you half-remember. And when you are reusing your own earlier text — from a thesis, a protocol, an earlier paper — cite it, because journals treat undisclosed reuse as a research integrity issue rather than a stylistic one. We screen for similarity before delivery and send the report with the draft, flagged by source, so you can see what is quoted and what is coincidental."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a literature review be published as a standalone paper?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is a legitimate route to a citable output, though the bar has moved. Narrative reviews are increasingly asked to say how the literature was found, which is why SANRA exists as a quality scale for them and why many journals now expect at least a described search. If you want the review to carry weight, the choice is to make it systematic in method even if narrative in voice: state the databases, the dates, the inclusion criteria and the number of records, and report under PRISMA where it applies. A review that says how it found what it found is cited more and disputed less."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Literature review and gap analysis","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/literature-review-and-gap/"}]}];

